'use strict';

window.OPTIVER_VI_1_DATA=[{"id":115001,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Interviewer Introduction and Transition to the Exercise","displayTitle":"Interviewer Introduction and Transition to the Exercise","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 00:00–00:45\n\nThe interviewer described his work as a semi-systematic trader, including trading systems, pricing automation, scaling processes across equities, and low-latency exchange interaction. He then asked you to explain briefly why Optiver and trading fit you.","solution":"### What happened\n\nThe interviewer gave useful clues about the role before asking the first question: the work combines systems, pricing, equities, automation, and low-latency execution. This was an opportunity to mirror two or three of those themes in your answer.\n\n### Your behavior\n\nYou opened by praising his experience and then moved into your own background. The tone was friendly, but the praise consumed some of the short opening window and did not answer the question.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You sounded engaged rather than detached.\n- You listened to his introduction.\n- You transitioned into your background without a long silence.\n\n### What to improve\n\nDo not begin with a general compliment such as “your experience is amazing.” Use the interviewer’s introduction as evidence that you understand the role. Select only the themes that connect to you.\n\n### Better response pattern\n\n“Your description of combining systematic research with live pricing and exchange interaction is exactly what attracts me to trading. My background in mathematics and financial engineering gives me the quantitative foundation, and my equity index-rebalancing project taught me to turn incomplete data into a usable trading signal.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Listening and engagement: 7/10\n- Directness: 5/10\n- Use of interviewer context: 4/10\n\nThe stage was acceptable, but it could have created a much stronger first impression with a direct connection to the interviewer’s own description.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 00:00–00:45","row":1}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115002,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Why Optiver and Why Trading?","displayTitle":"Why Optiver and Why Trading?","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 00:45–01:45\n\nBriefly explain why you, Optiver, and quantitative trading are a good fit.","solution":"### Your answer\n\nYou said you graduated from the University of Hong Kong with a mathematics major, were pursuing financial engineering at Cornell, and were a problem solver by nature. You highlighted your Dimensional Asia Capital index-rebalancing project: you had to construct a Python pipeline without being given a direct index-data feed and achieved a reported 82.6% hit rate.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You presented a coherent quantitative background.\n- You used a specific project rather than speaking only in abstractions.\n- The missing-data-feed constraint showed independence and resourcefulness.\n- The 82.6% statistic made the result concrete.\n\n### What weakened the answer\n\nThe answer mainly established that you can solve quantitative problems. It did not fully establish why you want trading, why Optiver specifically, or why you enjoy making decisions under uncertainty. “I am a problem solver” is too broad unless connected to real-time decisions and feedback.\n\nThe reported hit rate can also invite difficult questions. You should be prepared to define the event being predicted, the denominator, the test period, and whether the result was genuinely out of sample.\n\n### Stronger answer\n\n“I am interested in trading because it combines quantitative reasoning with fast decisions under uncertainty and immediate feedback. My mathematics degree trained me to decompose difficult problems, and Cornell’s financial-engineering program is helping me apply that reasoning to markets. At Dimensional Asia Capital, I built an equity index-rebalancing pipeline without a direct index feed and produced an 82.6% hit rate on the defined rebalance events. What I liked most was turning an imperfect-data problem into a decision tool. Optiver appeals to me because its trading culture emphasizes probability, rapid feedback, and continuous improvement.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Evidence and specificity: 8/10\n- Why trading: 5/10\n- Why Optiver: 4/10\n- Overall: 6/10\n\nThis was a credible answer with good raw material, but it did not directly complete all parts of the question.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 00:45–01:45","row":2}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115003,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Index-Rebalancing Follow-up: Asset Class and Project Precision","displayTitle":"Index-Rebalancing Follow-up: Asset Class and Project Precision","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 01:45–02:08\n\nThe interviewer asked which asset class your index-rebalancing research covered.","solution":"### Your answer\n\nYou answered that the project concerned stocks and explained that you predicted additions and deletions among index constituents.\n\n### What was effective\n\nThe answer was correct, concise, and immediately resolved the ambiguity. You did not over-explain after the interviewer indicated that it made sense.\n\n### What to improve\n\nUse the professional term “equities” first, then specify the exact task. If possible, distinguish prediction from execution.\n\n### Better answer\n\n“It was an equities project. I predicted which stocks would be added to or deleted from the index at a rebalance, then structured the output so it could support positioning around the rebalance event.”\n\n### Follow-ups to prepare\n\n- What information set was available before announcement?\n- How was the 82.6% hit rate defined?\n- How did you avoid look-ahead bias?\n- Were additions and deletions scored separately?\n- What was the estimated P&L after costs and crowding?\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Accuracy: 8/10\n- Concision: 8/10\n- Technical precision: 6.5/10\n\nThis was one of the cleanest exchanges in the interview.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 01:45–02:08","row":3}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115004,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Entering the Assessment Portal and Maintaining Composure","displayTitle":"Entering the Assessment Portal and Maintaining Composure","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 02:09–06:43\n\nThe interviewer paused briefly, returned, and asked you to enter the assessment portal and join the card-game interview.","solution":"### What happened\n\nThere was a several-minute interruption and then some friction locating and logging into the assessment portal. You asked whether you should use the candidate portal and confirmed the “join interview” action.\n\n### Your behavior\n\nYou remained polite, repeatedly said there was no problem, and did not express frustration. Once the interviewer returned, you worked through the login steps aloud.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You stayed calm during dead time.\n- You did not blame the platform or interviewer.\n- You confirmed the correct portal before proceeding.\n\n### What to improve\n\nRepeated filler such as “no problem” and “okay” is harmless but can make you sound nervous. During technical setup, use short operational statements: “I’m opening the candidate portal now,” “I see the join button,” and “I’m connected.”\n\n### Better behavior\n\nUse the interruption to prepare a blank worksheet with four sections: prior, known cards, fair value, and inventory. That would have reduced later arithmetic and bookkeeping errors.\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Professionalism: 8/10\n- Efficiency: 6/10\n- Preparation during downtime: 4/10\n\nThe behavior was professional, but the unused downtime was a missed opportunity to prepare a clean calculation structure.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 02:09–06:43","row":4}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115005,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Understand and Restate the Card-Game Rules","displayTitle":"Understand and Restate the Card-Game Rules","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 06:43–10:15\n\nThe interviewer explained a nine-card trading game with private cards, four community cards, four CPU counterparties, signed card values, sequential bid/ask markets, and one public reveal after each round.","solution":"### Reconstructed rules\n\n- The modified deck contains ranks Ace through King in three suits, for 39 cards total.\n- Rank magnitudes are \\(10,20,\\ldots,130\\).\n- Spades and clubs are positive; hearts are negative; diamonds are absent.\n- There are 26 positive cards and 13 negative cards.\n- Nine cards determine settlement: your private card, four CPU private cards, and four community cards.\n- Each CPU shows a bid, ask, and maximum volume. You may sell at the bid, buy at the ask, or pass.\n- After trading against all four CPUs, one community card is revealed to everyone.\n- Four rounds are played until all four community cards are public.\n\n### Your behavior\n\nYou asked what the community cards were used for, whether the market concerned the sum of all nine cards, whether only your own card was initially known, and whether the community cards stayed hidden. These questions eventually produced the correct mental model.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You did not pretend to understand unclear rules.\n- You correctly restated that the traded object was the sum of all nine cards.\n- You confirmed the reveal sequence before risking money.\n\n### What to improve\n\nThe questions came one at a time and occasionally repeated already stated information. After the first explanation, give one complete restatement and ask the interviewer to correct it.\n\n### Better response\n\n“Let me restate the game. Settlement is the signed sum of nine cards. I know my private card, each CPU knows its own, and four community cards begin hidden. I trade sequentially against four CPU bid/ask markets, then one community card is revealed to everyone. We repeat for four rounds. Is that correct?”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Willingness to clarify: 8/10\n- Speed of rule acquisition: 5.5/10\n- Final understanding: 7/10\n\nClarifying was correct, but a structured restatement would have been faster and more confident.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 06:43–10:15","row":5}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115006,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Calculate the Prior Expected Value of One Unknown Card","displayTitle":"Calculate the Prior Expected Value of One Unknown Card","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 11:06–13:38\n\nBefore trading, calculate the expected signed value of an unknown card from the modified deck.","solution":"### Your calculation\n\nYou identified the mean unsigned magnitude as 70 and tried to combine the \\(2/3\\) positive probability with the \\(1/3\\) negative probability. Your spoken fractions were sometimes reversed—“3 over 2” and “3 over 1”—but you arrived at the correct numerical mean of approximately 23.3.\n\n### Correct calculation\n\nLet \\(M\\) be the magnitude. Since the thirteen magnitudes are equally likely,\n\n\\[\nE[M]=\\frac{10+130}{2}=70.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(S\\) be the sign. Then\n\n\\[\nP(S=1)=\\frac23,\n\\qquad\nP(S=-1)=\\frac13.\n\\]\n\nBecause suit and rank are independent in the deck,\n\n\\[\nE[X]=E[S]E[M]\n=\\left(\\frac23-\\frac13\\right)70\n=\\frac{70}{3}\n\\approx23.33.\n\\]\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You immediately searched for an expected-value baseline.\n- You correctly used the average of an arithmetic sequence.\n- You reached the correct numerical result.\n\n### What to improve\n\nDo not say “3 over 2” when you mean “2 over 3.” In a trading interview, an incorrect spoken fraction undermines confidence even if the final number is right. Write the probabilities before multiplying.\n\n### Better response\n\n“The average magnitude is 70. The expected sign is \\(2/3-1/3=1/3\\), so an unknown card contributes \\(70/3=23.33\\) points on average.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Concept: 8/10\n- Arithmetic: 7/10\n- Verbal precision: 4.5/10\n- Overall: 6.5/10\n\nThe mathematical idea was correct; the presentation made it look less reliable than it was.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 11:06–13:38","row":6}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115007,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Initial Fair Value with Your Red Six","displayTitle":"Initial Fair Value with Your Red Six","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 11:20–14:08\n\nYour private card was a red 6, worth \\(-60\\). Eight other cards were unknown. What was the initial fair value of the nine-card sum?","solution":"### Your answer\n\nYou multiplied the 23.3 prior by eight and combined it with your \\(-60\\) card. You first said roughly 125, then explained the result as roughly 126.\n\n### Correct calculation\n\n\\[\nF_0=-60+8\\left(\\frac{70}{3}\\right)\n=-60+\\frac{560}{3}\n=\\frac{380}{3}\n\\approx126.67.\n\\]\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You counted eight unknown cards correctly.\n- You treated your private red card as negative.\n- Your estimate was close enough for an initial trading value.\n\n### What to improve\n\nKeep the exact fractional expression until the final step. Repeatedly rounding 23.3 creates avoidable drift. State one number and keep it on the worksheet.\n\n### Better response\n\n“My private card is \\(-60\\). The other eight cards contribute \\(8\\times70/3=186.67\\), so my initial fair value is \\(126.67\\). I’ll use 127 as my working value.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Setup: 8/10\n- Card counting: 8/10\n- Arithmetic stability: 6/10\n- Overall: 7/10\n\nThis was substantively correct and one of the foundations you handled well.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 11:20–14:08","row":7}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115008,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"CPU 1 at 213/233: Bid/Ask Direction","displayTitle":"CPU 1 at 213/233: Bid/Ask Direction","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 14:09–15:33\n\nCPU 1 displayed 213 bid / 233 ask with volume one. Explain the available actions and choose a trade using your current fair value.","solution":"### Your behavior\n\nYou initially said you would “buy the bid.” The interviewer stopped you and clarified that CPU 1 buys at 213: you sell to its bid. You asked again which button to press, then sold one at 213.\n\n### Correct market mechanics\n\nFor a quote \\(b/a\\):\n\n- hit the bid \\(b\\) to sell;\n- lift the ask \\(a\\) to buy;\n- pass to do nothing.\n\nWith working fair value \\(F_0=126.67\\), selling at 213 has mark-to-fair edge\n\n\\[\n213-126.67=86.33.\n\\]\n\nBuying at 233 has edge\n\n\\[\n126.67-233=-106.33.\n\\]\n\nUnder the naive prior, sell one at 213.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- After correction, you executed the economically correct direction.\n- You asked for clarification rather than clicking randomly.\n\n### What hurt the performance\n\nBid/ask direction is basic trading fluency. Requiring multiple clarifications likely had a meaningful negative effect because the role is trading-focused.\n\n### What to do instead\n\nBefore any game, write: “SELL → BID | ASK ← BUY.” Then say: “My value is 127, below the 213 bid, so I sell one.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Economic decision after clarification: 7/10\n- Independent market mechanics: 3/10\n- Communication: 4/10\n- Overall: 4.5/10\n\nThis was probably one of the most damaging early mistakes, although you recovered.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 14:09–15:33","row":8}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115009,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Infer CPU 1’s Private Card from Its Quote","displayTitle":"Infer CPU 1’s Private Card from Its Quote","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 15:34–18:25\n\nCPU 1’s market was much higher than your initial fair value. Use the quote to infer its private card and update your valuation.","solution":"### Your reasoning\n\nYou realized that CPU 1’s high quote must reflect information in its private card. You estimated that it held approximately \\(+40\\), then updated your fair value from roughly 126 to roughly 143.\n\n### Clean derivation\n\nIf CPU 1 knows only its private card \\(c_1\\) and treats the other eight cards as unknown, its fair value is approximately\n\n\\[\nm_1=c_1+8\\mu,\n\\qquad \\mu=70/3.\n\\]\n\nUsing the quote midpoint,\n\n\\[\nm_1=\\frac{213+233}{2}=223.\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nc_1\\approx223-8\\left(\\frac{70}{3}\\right)\n=223-186.67\n=36.33,\n\\]\n\nsuggesting the nearest valid card value \\(+40\\).\n\nFrom your perspective, after replacing one unknown-card prior by \\(+40\\),\n\n\\[\nF_1=-60+40+7\\left(\\frac{70}{3}\\right)\n=143.33.\n\\]\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You recognized prices as information, which is central to the exercise.\n- Your inferred \\(+40\\) and updated fair value near 143 were coherent.\n- You explained the conditioning logic rather than treating the quote as arbitrary.\n\n### What to improve\n\nUse the midpoint explicitly. Do not infer a precise card without stating assumptions about CPU rationality, spread construction, and inventory.\n\n### Better response\n\n“CPU 1’s midpoint is 223. Subtracting the prior value of its eight unknown cards, 186.67, implies a private card near +36, so +40 is the closest deck value. My updated fair value is 143.33.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Trading intuition: 8/10\n- Mathematical structure: 7/10\n- Precision and speed: 5.5/10\n- Overall: 7/10\n\nThis was an important positive signal in the interview.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 15:34–18:25","row":9}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115010,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"CPU 2: Turn a Large Edge into a Quantitative Decision","displayTitle":"CPU 2: Turn a Large Edge into a Quantitative Decision","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 18:25–21:15\n\nCPU 2 quoted a market with a bid around 261. Your current fair value was around 143. The interviewer asked whether you could be more quantitative and more confident in trading.","solution":"### Your behavior\n\nYou first wanted to pass because you were still gathering information and were concerned about becoming short. The interviewer highlighted the large gap between your 143 fair value and the 261 bid and asked whether the situation could be expressed more quantitatively. You then inferred a large positive CPU 2 card, updated your value to roughly 209, and sold one because the bid still exceeded your estimate.\n\n### What the interviewer was testing\n\nThe interviewer wanted you to convert an estimate into an action and size, not merely narrate uncertainty. Even after accounting for CPU 2’s private information, the bid could remain above your conditional fair value.\n\n### Better calculation pattern\n\n1. Compute midpoint \\(m_2\\).\n2. Infer \\(c_2\\approx m_2-8\\mu\\).\n3. Replace one prior unknown contribution \\(\\mu\\) with \\(c_2\\).\n4. Compare the revised fair value with the executable bid.\n5. State edge, model risk, and size.\n\nIf the revised value is about 209 and the bid is 261,\n\n\\[\n\\text{sell edge}=261-209=52.\n\\]\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You changed your mind when the interviewer exposed the inconsistency.\n- You eventually quantified the edge and sold.\n- You recognized that quote information must be incorporated before trading.\n\n### What to improve\n\n“Gather more information” is not a sufficient reason to pass when the current edge is very large. Inventory is a real concern, but quantify it separately. Do not let vague risk aversion replace the expected-value comparison.\n\n### Better response\n\n“After inferring CPU 2’s card, my value rises to about 209. Its 261 bid is still 52 points above my value, so I sell the available unit. I accept the model risk because the edge is large relative to reasonable inference error.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Initial decisiveness: 3.5/10\n- Response to prompting: 7/10\n- Final quantitative decision: 7/10\n- Overall: 5.5/10\n\nThe final action was defensible, but the interviewer had to push you from a qualitative hesitation into the calculation.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 18:25–21:15","row":10}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115011,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"CPU 3 and CPU 4: Sequential Bayesian Updating","displayTitle":"CPU 3 and CPU 4: Sequential Bayesian Updating","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 21:15–24:56\n\nInfer the remaining CPU private cards from their markets, update the nine-card fair value, and decide whether to trade.","solution":"### Your reasoning\n\nYou inferred approximately \\(+80\\) for CPU 3 and \\(+50\\) for CPU 4. Combined with earlier estimates \\(+40\\) and \\(+90\\), you updated your total fair value to about 266 after CPU 3 and about 293 after CPU 4. You passed CPU 3 because its market contained your fair value and bought from CPU 4 because its ask of 249 was below your estimated 293.\n\n### Correct consolidated calculation\n\nTaking the reconstructed private-card estimates as \\(+40,+90,+80,+50\\), your known-card sum is\n\n\\[\n-60+40+90+80+50=200.\n\\]\n\nFour community cards remain unknown, so\n\n\\[\nF=200+4\\left(\\frac{70}{3}\\right)\n=200+93.33\n=293.33.\n\\]\n\nIf CPU 4 asks 249, estimated edge is\n\n\\[\n293.33-249=44.33,\n\\]\n\nso buying is consistent with the model.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You updated after every new quote rather than freezing the original prior.\n- You understood that a market containing fair value usually implies no immediate trade.\n- You identified a buy when ask was below fair value.\n- Your final 293 estimate was internally coherent.\n\n### What to improve\n\nThe arithmetic was slow and restarted several times. At points you said “plus 15 50,” “93,” and then “293,” making the reasoning difficult to audit. Maintain a table with columns: CPU, midpoint, inferred card, revised known sum, unknown count, fair value.\n\n### Better response\n\n“My inferred private cards are +40, +90, +80, and +50. Together with my −60, the known sum is 200. Four community cards remain, worth 93.33 in expectation, so fair value is 293.33. CPU 4 offers 249, giving 44.33 points of edge; I buy one.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Sequential updating concept: 8/10\n- Trading decisions: 7.5/10\n- Arithmetic control: 4.5/10\n- Communication: 4.5/10\n- Overall: 6.5/10\n\nThe underlying idea was strong, but execution looked less reliable than the final numbers.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 21:15–24:56","row":11}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115012,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"After the Red Four Reveal: Update Fair Value","displayTitle":"After the Red Four Reveal: Update Fair Value","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 24:57–28:15\n\nThe first community card was revealed as a red 4, worth \\(-40\\). Update your fair value and explain the quote shift you expect.","solution":"### Your answer\n\nYou eventually computed the updated fair value as 230. You also reasoned that replacing an unknown expected card with \\(-40\\) should cause a substantial downward move in CPU quotes.\n\n### Correct fair value\n\nThe previously inferred known private-card total was 200. Adding the revealed \\(-40\\) community card gives 160. Three community cards remain unknown:\n\n\\[\nF=160+3\\left(\\frac{70}{3}\\right)\n=160+70\n=\\boxed{230}.\n\\]\n\n### Expected quote movement\n\nBefore reveal, the card contributed expected value \\(\\mu=23.33\\). After reveal, it contributes \\(-40\\). Therefore the public-information shock is\n\n\\[\n-40-23.33=-63.33.\n\\]\n\nIf this is the only new information and the CPU pricing rule is otherwise unchanged, its quote midpoint should fall by approximately 63.33.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- The final updated fair value was exactly right.\n- You understood the difference between the realized card value and its previous expectation.\n- You began comparing observed quote changes with a quantitative benchmark.\n\n### What to improve\n\nYou recounted the known cards aloud several times. Use the known-sum method: previous known sum 200, add −40, then add three priors. This reduces card-count errors.\n\n### Better response\n\n“The reveal replaces a +23.33 prior contribution with −40, a −63.33 shock. My known sum becomes 160 and three cards remain unknown, so fair value is 230.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Final result: 9/10\n- Update logic: 8/10\n- Efficiency: 5.5/10\n- Overall: 7.5/10\n\nThis was mathematically one of your better stages.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 24:57–28:15","row":12}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115013,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Determine What Information the CPUs Use","displayTitle":"Determine What Information the CPUs Use","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 25:04–27:15\n\nYou asked whether CPU 1 could see the other CPU quotes and whether it could see your private card. The interviewer said it could not see your card directly and behaved rationally using information available to it, but declined to reveal the exact strategy.","solution":"### Your behavior\n\nYou tried to establish the CPU information set directly. When the interviewer did not provide the full rule, you assumed CPU 1 did not see other CPU markets or your card and used the approximately 63-point quote drop as supporting evidence.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You recognized that inference depends on the agent’s information set.\n- You noticed that the quote response to the public red 4 could help test competing models.\n- You attempted to confirm or reject a strategy using observed behavior.\n\n### What to improve\n\nThe interviewer intentionally withheld the exact strategy. Once that happens, do not turn an unconfirmed assumption into a fact. Maintain hypotheses:\n\n- Model A: CPU uses only its private card and public cards.\n- Model B: CPU also observes previous quotes.\n- Model C: CPU incorporates your trades or inventory.\n\nThen identify what future observation would distinguish them.\n\n### Better response\n\n“I cannot identify its information set from one quote. My benchmark model predicts a 63.33 drop from the public card alone. If CPU 1 moves by that amount while later CPUs show additional residual changes after observing prior activity, that would support an information-flow hypothesis. I’ll keep the model provisional.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Strategic curiosity: 8/10\n- Experimental reasoning: 6.5/10\n- Discipline about assumptions: 4.5/10\n- Overall: 6/10\n\nThe question was insightful; the conclusion was more confident than the evidence allowed.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 25:04–27:15","row":13}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115014,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Five Units Now or Five Units Next Round?","displayTitle":"Five Units Now or Five Units Next Round?","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 28:15–32:57\n\nThe interviewer offered five units against CPU 1 either in the current round or the next round. Which should you choose, and does the next public card change the valuation gap?","solution":"### Your answer\n\nYou chose the current round. You argued that CPU 1 would receive more information next round, its estimate would move closer to the true value, and the gap between your fair value and its market would narrow. The interviewer repeatedly emphasized that both you and CPU 1 would observe the same next community card.\n\n### Core distinction\n\nMore public information reduces uncertainty about settlement. It does not necessarily reduce a difference caused by private information.\n\nLet the next public card be \\(Y\\) and its prior mean be \\(\\mu\\). If both parties observe it, then\n\n\\[\nF_{\\text{you,next}}=F_{\\text{you,now}}+(Y-\\mu)\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nF_{\\text{CPU,next}}=F_{\\text{CPU,now}}+(Y-\\mu).\n\\]\n\nSubtracting,\n\n\\[\nF_{\\text{you,next}}-F_{\\text{CPU,next}}\n=F_{\\text{you,now}}-F_{\\text{CPU,now}}.\n\\]\n\nUnder this simple model, the expected edge is invariant to the common reveal. You should be indifferent between the two rounds unless inventory, risk, changing volume, strategic behavior, or quote rules create another difference.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You recognized a genuine advantage and wanted to trade larger size.\n- You tried to reason about how information affects prices.\n- You maintained your choice consistently.\n\n### What hurt the response\n\nYou did not reach the interviewer’s intended common-information insight despite several prompts. You conflated convergence to the final settlement with convergence between two conditional expectations.\n\n### Better response\n\n“Because the next card is public to both of us, it shifts both valuations by the same surprise \\(Y-\\mu\\). The gap created by different private information should remain unchanged, so I am indifferent between five units now and next round under the basic model. I would choose now only if I expect liquidity to disappear or want to avoid future execution risk.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Recognition of trading opportunity: 7/10\n- Information reasoning: 3.5/10\n- Response to interviewer guidance: 4/10\n- Overall: 4.5/10\n\nThis was a central conceptual miss and likely carried significant weight.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 28:15–32:57","row":14}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115015,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Understand Quote Size and Execute the Five-Unit Trade","displayTitle":"Understand Quote Size and Execute the Five-Unit Trade","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 32:45–33:23\n\nThe interviewer changed CPU 1’s quote size to five. How many units were available, and how should you execute?","solution":"### Your behavior\n\nYou asked whether entering five when the displayed quote size was five meant 25 units. The interviewer clarified that the maximum trade was five units. You then bought five.\n\n### Correct interpretation\n\nA quote size of five means that up to five units are available at the displayed price. It is a capacity, not a multiplier applied again to the entered quantity.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You checked before executing an unintended size.\n- You ultimately used the available size consistent with your estimated edge.\n\n### What to improve\n\nThe confusion reinforced the earlier impression that market-interface mechanics were unfamiliar. Before clicking, verbalize: “Quote size five means I can trade 0–5 units. I choose five.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Risk control: 7/10\n- Interface fluency: 3.5/10\n- Final execution: 7/10\n- Overall: 5/10\n\nClarifying prevented a mistake, but this was another basic mechanics issue in a trading interview.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 32:45–33:23","row":15}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115016,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Explain CPU 2’s Smaller-Than-Expected Quote Drop","displayTitle":"Explain CPU 2’s Smaller-Than-Expected Quote Drop","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 33:23–38:07\n\nCPU 2’s market fell by roughly 50 after the red 4 reveal, rather than the approximately 63.33 predicted by the public-card shock alone. Explain the residual.","solution":"### Your answer\n\nYou observed that CPU 2’s quote remained higher than your benchmark. You proposed that CPU 2 might have incorporated other CPUs’ quotes or your previous trades. When the interviewer clarified the earlier displayed market, you compared the actual move with the predicted 63.33 decline. Later, you agreed that a roughly 10–13 point difference could be consistent with additional information from high private cards.\n\n### Quantitative residual\n\nLet \\(m_{2,0}\\) and \\(m_{2,1}\\) be CPU 2’s midpoints before and after the reveal. The public-card-only model predicts\n\n\\[\nm_{2,1}-m_{2,0}\\approx-63.33.\n\\]\n\nIf the observed move is about \\(-50\\), the residual is\n\n\\[\nr=(-50)-(-63.33)=+13.33.\n\\]\n\n### Correct interpretation\n\nThe residual is evidence of an offsetting update or a model imperfection. It does not uniquely identify the cause. Possible explanations include learning from other markets, observing your trades, inventory skew, strategic quote shading, or rounding.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You created a quantitative counterfactual.\n- You looked for an explanation rather than ignoring the discrepancy.\n- You eventually recognized that the residual’s magnitude was not enormous.\n\n### What to improve\n\nState the residual before telling a story. Use “consistent with” rather than “I believe CPU 2 is looking at...” unless the behavior identifies the mechanism.\n\n### Better response\n\n“The red 4 alone predicts a 63.33 drop, while the observed drop is about 50, leaving a +13.33 residual. That indicates an offsetting update or quote adjustment. It is consistent with learning from other activity, but one observation does not identify the mechanism.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Benchmark construction: 8/10\n- Statistical discipline: 5/10\n- Final interpretation: 6.5/10\n- Overall: 6.5/10\n\nThis was a valuable piece of analysis, weakened mainly by overconfidence in the proposed mechanism.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 33:23–38:07","row":16}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115017,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Manage Inventory Without Letting It Replace Expected Value","displayTitle":"Manage Inventory Without Letting It Replace Expected Value","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 38:07–39:15\n\nAfter buying five and considering later markets, you noted that you were long and wanted to sell or avoid adding more risk. How should position management enter the decision?","solution":"### Your behavior\n\nYou bought one from a market whose ask was below your 230 fair value, noticed that you were long by five, and then passed CPU 4 partly because you were already too long.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You tracked the sign of your inventory.\n- You understood that a positive expected-value trade can still increase risk.\n- You avoided mechanically buying every favorable-looking offer.\n\n### What to improve\n\n“I'm long too many” is not a quantitative rule. Distinguish expected edge from inventory penalty. A simple risk-adjusted decision is\n\n\\[\n\\text{adjusted edge}\n=\\text{raw edge}-\\lambda\\,\\Delta\\text{risk},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda\\) represents risk aversion or a desk limit. Without a stated limit, passing may look inconsistent.\n\n### Better response\n\n“My fair value implies positive edge, but I am already long five. Another unit increases exposure to the unrevealed cards. If my position limit is five, I pass; otherwise I compare the edge with the incremental variance before trading.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Inventory awareness: 7/10\n- Quantification of risk: 3.5/10\n- Consistency: 5/10\n- Overall: 5/10\n\nThe instinct was sensible, but the decision rule needed to be explicit.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 38:07–39:15","row":17}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115018,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"First Value-of-Information Question","displayTitle":"First Value-of-Information Question","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 39:15–41:37\n\nBefore the third community card was public, the interviewer offered to show it privately before the CPUs. How much should you pay for that information?","solution":"### Your response\n\nYou first recalculated fair value and looked at existing P&L. You then paused, said you were unsure, and asked for a hint. The interviewer suggested comparing the trades you would make without the card with the trades you could make after seeing it, then valuing the difference. Because time was short, the round continued without a completed answer.\n\n### What the question was testing\n\nThis is expected value of sample information, not the expected value of the card itself and not current P&L.\n\nLet \\(C\\) be the hidden card and \\(a\\) a feasible trading action. The maximum fair price is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{EVSI}\n=E_C\\!\\left[\\max_a E[\\Pi(a)\\mid C]\\right]\n-\\max_a E_C\\!\\left[E[\\Pi(a)\\mid C]\\right].\n\\]\n\n### Practical enumeration\n\nFor each of the 39 equally likely physical cards:\n\n1. update fair value by \\(c-\\mu\\);\n2. compare the updated value with every current bid and ask;\n3. choose the best allowed trades and sizes;\n4. record profit;\n5. average across cards;\n6. subtract profit from the best decision made without seeing the card.\n\nFor one CPU with bid \\(b\\), ask \\(a\\), volume \\(q\\), and conditional value \\(F_c\\), the best unconstrained mark-to-fair profit is\n\n\\[\nq\\max\\{b-F_c,\\;F_c-a,\\;0\\}.\n\\]\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You recognized that the answer should concern the economic benefit of information.\n- You asked for a hint rather than inventing a confident number immediately.\n\n### What to improve\n\nYou mixed fair value, P&L, and information value. State the EVSI definition first, even if there is not enough time to enumerate. That alone demonstrates the correct concept.\n\n### Better response\n\n“I will compare the optimal trade set without the card against the optimal trade set for each possible card value, average the latter over the 39 cards, and take the difference. That difference is the most I should pay.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Recognition of concept: 4.5/10\n- Completed calculation: 2/10\n- Use of hint: 5/10\n- Overall: 3.5/10\n\nThis was another major technical weakness in the round.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 39:15–41:37","row":18}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115019,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Rapid Decisions in the Penultimate Round","displayTitle":"Rapid Decisions in the Penultimate Round","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 41:37–42:14\n\nWith limited time, you compared your fair value of roughly 216 with the CPU markets and made quick buy/pass decisions.","solution":"### Your actions\n\nYou said your value was about 216, bought when an offer was below that level, passed a market around 220, passed another market you considered not valuable enough, and bought from CPU 4.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You began making decisions faster after the interviewer noted the time pressure.\n- You compared executable prices with fair value.\n- Passing near 220 when value was about 216 was consistent.\n\n### What to improve\n\nState the price, fair value, edge, and action in one line for each CPU. The audio does not preserve every displayed quote clearly, so the exact profitability of all four actions cannot be independently verified.\n\n### Better response format\n\n- “CPU 1 ask is \\(a_1\\); \\(216-a_1\\) is positive, so buy one.”\n- “CPU 2 bid/ask straddles 216, so pass.”\n- “CPU 3 edge is below my buffer, so pass.”\n- “CPU 4 ask is below 216 by \\(e_4\\), so buy one.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Speed: 7/10\n- Decision structure: 6/10\n- Auditability: 4.5/10\n- Overall: 5.5/10\n\nYou improved the pace, but explicit edge statements would have made the decisions easier to evaluate.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 41:37–42:14","row":19}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115020,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Final Public Card and the Flat-Round Constraint","displayTitle":"Final Public Card and the Flat-Round Constraint","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 42:14–43:22\n\nA negative 10 card was revealed. The interviewer then required you to finish the final round flat: any quantity bought during the round had to be sold during that same round. Clarify the constraint and design the strategy.","solution":"### Your behavior\n\nYou began updating fair value, then stopped when the new constraint was introduced. You correctly asked whether “flat” meant flat only within the final round or flat across the entire game. After the interviewer confirmed it applied only to the round, you proposed buying from lower-priced CPU 1 and selling to higher-priced CPU 2.\n\n### Correct flat-trade logic\n\nBuy \\(q\\) units at ask \\(a_i\\) and sell the same quantity at bid \\(b_j\\). The final settlement value \\(V\\) cancels:\n\n\\[\n\\Pi=q(V-a_i)+q(b_j-V)=q(b_j-a_i).\n\\]\n\nThe trade is profitable exactly when\n\n\\[\nb_j>a_i.\n\\]\n\nChoose the lowest available ask and highest available bid, with quantity limited by both quote sizes and the round constraint.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- Your clarification of “flat” was precise and necessary.\n- You immediately recognized the cross-market buy-low/sell-high structure.\n- This was a stronger response than several earlier stages because it focused on executable prices.\n\n### What to improve\n\nState that settlement risk cancels. Without that sentence, the strategy sounds intuitive rather than proven.\n\n### Better response\n\n“Because I must end the round with zero net change in position, I pair buys and sells. The settlement cancels, so profit is sell bid minus buy ask. I buy from the lowest ask and sell to the highest bid, subject to matched volume.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Constraint clarification: 8/10\n- Strategy: 8/10\n- Formal explanation: 5.5/10\n- Overall: 7/10\n\nThis was one of your strongest behavioral and trading responses.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 42:14–43:22","row":20}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115021,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Second Information-Value Question Under the Flat Constraint","displayTitle":"Second Information-Value Question Under the Flat Constraint","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 43:22–46:10\n\nBefore the final trades, the interviewer again offered advance knowledge of the community card and asked what you would pay.","solution":"### Your response\n\nYou estimated that buying from CPU 1 and selling to CPU 2 would earn around 60. You then said knowing the card might earn another 60 and initially offered 60. After the interviewer questioned the reasoning, you revised the amount to 30 and acknowledged that the calculation was rough.\n\n### Correct special-case insight\n\nUnder fixed quotes and a same-round flat constraint, every matched trade has payoff\n\n\\[\nq(b_j-a_i),\n\\]\n\nwhich does not depend on settlement \\(V\\). If the card does not change available quotes or volumes, seeing it cannot change the optimal matched trade. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\operatorname{EVSI}=0}.\n\\]\n\nIf the card would be shown before CPUs set new quotes, or if non-flat trades were permitted, information could have positive value. The timing matters.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- You attempted to link the information price to incremental trading profit.\n- You admitted uncertainty instead of defending an unsupported value indefinitely.\n\n### What hurt the response\n\nThe proposed 60 and revised 30 were not derived. More importantly, the flat constraint should have triggered the realization that settlement cancels. This was likely the interviewer’s intended connection between the two questions.\n\n### Better response\n\n“If the current quotes remain fixed and I must end flat, the card has zero value because both legs’ settlement exposure cancels. I would pay zero. If seeing the card occurs before new quotes are generated, I need to model how it changes those quotes.”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Attempt to value incrementally: 5/10\n- Mathematical conclusion: 2/10\n- Recognition after prompting: 3/10\n- Overall: 3/10\n\nThis was probably the clearest missed opportunity near the end of the interview.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 43:22–46:10","row":21}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115022,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Card Game","question":"Thirty-Second Final Execution","displayTitle":"Thirty-Second Final Execution","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 46:10–46:50\n\nThe interviewer gave you roughly 30 seconds to execute the final round while respecting the flat constraint.","solution":"### Your final action\n\nAfter a short pause, you stated: “buy, sell, pass, pass.” The interviewer accepted the sequence and ended the game.\n\n### What was effective\n\n- The sequence appears consistent with the strategy of buying from the lower-priced CPU and selling to the higher-priced CPU.\n- One buy and one sell kept the round’s net position flat.\n- You complied with the time limit and did not continue revising the decision.\n\n### What to improve\n\nGive the quantities and locked-in spread. “Buy one from CPU 1 at its ask; sell one to CPU 2 at its bid; locked profit is \\(b_2-a_1\\); pass CPUs 3 and 4.” That proves constraint compliance and expected profit.\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Constraint compliance: 8/10\n- Speed: 7/10\n- Explanation: 5/10\n- Overall: 6.5/10\n\nThe final execution recovered some ground, even though the preceding information-value answer was weak.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 46:10–46:50","row":22}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115023,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Closing Questions","question":"Candidate Questions and Interviewer’s Advice","displayTitle":"Candidate Questions and Interviewer’s Advice","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 46:50–49:05\n\nYou asked about the next recruiting stage and what quality makes an Optiver intern stand out. Record the interviewer’s answers and evaluate the questions.","solution":"### Your first question\n\nYou asked when you would hear back and what format the next stage would take. The interviewer said recruiting would communicate, probably within roughly a week, but he was uncertain about the exact format and thought it might involve another assessment or video interview.\n\n### Evaluation\n\nThis was a reasonable logistical question, but the interviewer was not the best source. Recruiters can answer it more precisely.\n\n### Your second question\n\nYou asked what quality makes interns stand out beyond being smart.\n\n### Interviewer’s answer\n\nHe emphasized:\n\n- being open-minded and willing to learn;\n- understanding the business before making decisions;\n- asking why the firm currently does something a certain way;\n- preserving existing strengths while improving weaknesses;\n- moving rapidly up the learning curve;\n- improving during the internship rather than merely starting strong.\n\nHe specifically contrasted bringing a technique directly from school with first understanding why Optiver’s current process exists.\n\n### Evaluation\n\nThe second question was strong. It elicited a detailed, personal answer and revealed the exact behavior the interviewer values. It also suggests how he may have evaluated the card game: not only correctness, but whether you absorbed hints and improved your model.\n\n### Stronger closing questions\n\n- “What feedback most changed the way you approached trading when you joined?”\n- “What does rapid improvement look like during the first few weeks of the internship?”\n- “In this card exercise, what general habit would you recommend I practice?”\n\n### Stage evaluation\n\n- Logistics question: 5.5/10\n- Culture/quality question: 8.5/10\n- Listening to answer: 7/10\n- Overall: 7/10\n\nThe interview ended professionally, and the second question was a good choice.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 46:50–49:05","row":23}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true},{"id":115024,"category":"OPTIVER VIDEO INTERVIEW 1","originalCategory":"My Interview","company":"Optiver","role":"Quantitative Trading Interview · Full Review","question":"Complete Behavioral and Technical Performance Audit","displayTitle":"Complete Behavioral and Technical Performance Audit","questionBody":"Audio timestamp: 00:00–49:48\n\nEvaluate the entire interview stage by stage, including mathematical ability, market mechanics, learning behavior, communication, and the most important changes for the next interview.","solution":"### Strongest evidence from the recording\n\n- You correctly obtained the unknown-card prior \\(70/3\\).\n- You correctly structured the initial fair value and reached approximately 126.67.\n- You recognized that quotes contain information about private cards.\n- The inferred private-card sequence was coherent enough to yield fair values near 293.33 and 230.\n- You correctly measured the red 4 public-information shock as approximately \\(-63.33\\).\n- You tracked inventory and understood that position changes risk.\n- You correctly clarified the flat constraint and selected a buy/sell pair.\n- You remained polite, engaged, and willing to ask questions throughout.\n\n### Most important weaknesses\n\n1. **Market mechanics:** You reversed bid and ask and were uncertain about quote size. These are high-salience mistakes in a trading interview.\n2. **Arithmetic control:** Correct frameworks were obscured by repeated recalculation, inconsistent spoken fractions, and restarted totals.\n3. **Common versus private information:** You did not identify that a public card shifts both parties’ valuations equally under the simple model.\n4. **Value of information:** You did not complete the EVSI framework and later supplied unsupported prices of 60 and 30.\n5. **Assumption discipline:** You sometimes converted a plausible story about CPU behavior into a conclusion without enough identifying evidence.\n6. **Communication:** Many answers were long, recursive, and filled with self-correction. The interviewer repeatedly asked for a quantitative statement.\n7. **Response to hints:** You remained engaged, but did not always reach the intended insight after multiple prompts. This matters because the interviewer later said rapid learning is a key intern quality.\n\n### Recommended live worksheet\n\nMaintain a compact table:\n\n- prior per unknown card: \\(\\mu=70/3\\);\n- known signed-card sum;\n- number of unknown cards;\n- fair value \\(F=K+n_u\\mu\\);\n- each CPU midpoint and inferred private card;\n- current inventory;\n- executable edge for every bid and ask.\n\nFor each decision, say only:\n\n“My fair value is \\(F\\). The executable price is \\(P\\). Edge is \\(F-P\\) if buying or \\(P-F\\) if selling. My action is X units. The main model risk is Y.”\n\n### What to stop doing\n\n- Stop narrating every arithmetic attempt.\n- Stop using “probably,” “maybe,” and “I think” before every number.\n- Stop allowing inventory discomfort to replace a defined risk rule.\n- Stop naming a CPU strategy as fact when only one residual supports it.\n- Stop quoting an information price before defining the counterfactual actions.\n\n### What to do instead\n\n- Write exact fractions and round once.\n- Use quote midpoint to infer information.\n- Separate common information, private information, and strategic quote effects.\n- Define EVSI before calculating it.\n- Under a flat constraint, check whether settlement cancels.\n- Give the final number and action first, then one sentence of reasoning.\n\n### Stage-by-stage scorecard\n\n- Motivation and fit: 6/10\n- Rule acquisition: 6/10\n- Probability and expected value: 7/10\n- Bid/ask and quote-size fluency: 3.5/10\n- Inference from prices: 7/10\n- Arithmetic reliability: 5/10\n- Information structure: 4/10\n- Value-of-information reasoning: 3/10\n- Inventory awareness: 6/10\n- Flat-round strategy: 7/10\n- Communication efficiency: 4.5/10\n- Coachability during the exercise: 5.5/10\n- Professionalism: 8/10\n\n### Overall assessment\n\nThe interview showed genuine quantitative and trading intuition, especially when you inferred hidden private cards from markets. It also exposed gaps in trading mechanics, concise numerical communication, and information-value reasoning. The performance was neither a clear failure nor a clean pass. The best improvement would come from practicing short market-making games where every decision must be stated as fair value, executable edge, size, and one model-risk caveat.","kind":"Detailed audio reconstruction with stage evaluation","sources":[{"sheet":"Optiver card-game interview audio · timestamp 00:00–49:48","row":24}],"originalAnswer":"","note":null,"references":[],"privateInterview":true}];
